\textbf{Cross-baseline context (RQ2).} Against widely cited baselines reported under the same evaluation harness, the recommended VMAF-aware operating point reduces mean session rebuffering by \VRbReductionVsPen\,\%{} versus Pensieve (\VRbPen\,\%) and \VRbReductionVsMPC\,\%{} versus RobustMPC (\VRbMPC\,\%). Absolute QoE numbers are not directly paired across distinct trained policies and we therefore report them descriptively in Tables~\ref{tab:main:results}--\ref{tab:wilcoxon:rb} rather than as a strict claim; the dominant cross-baseline claim is the order-of-magnitude rebuffer reduction, paired QoE deltas relative to other learned policies appear in the supplementary statistical grids.

\textbf{Statistical reporting.} Tables~\ref{tab:wilcoxon:qoe}--\ref{tab:wilcoxon:rb} excerpt paired Wilcoxon outcomes; full grids (QoE, Rebuffer, VMAF) for every shield variant in the sweep are exported as CSV beside those fragments. The vmaf-aware-vs-legacy comparison (Table~\ref{tab:pareto:family}, rows 2--3 vs.\ row 1) is highlighted as the primary headline because positive paired $\Delta$QoE \emph{simultaneously} with negative paired $\Delta$rebuffer \emph{and} positive paired $\Delta$VMAF, all statistically significant, is, to our knowledge, unprecedented in the published ABR shield literature. For distributional-shift scenarios with extreme outage-driven rebuffer (Sec.~\ref{sec:eval:ood}), metrics should be quoted dimension-wise: paired QoE and stall shifts remain primary evidence; perceptual deltas may require larger \(n\) or equivalence-style tests~\cite{lakens2018equivalence} if VMAF-noninferiority claims are needed.

\subsection*{Shield-Design-Space Collapse (RQ4)}
\label{sec:eval:shields}

\textbf{Sweep design.} We instantiate $\VShieldVariants$ shield configurations spanning four orthogonal axes: (i)~the catastrophic ratio $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}\in\{2,3,4,5,\infty\}$; (ii)~VMAF-aware fallback on/off, with VMAF-loss budget $\tau\in\{1,4,8\}$; (iii)~$H$-step lookahead rollout pre-gating with $H\in\{1,2,3\}$ and minimum-buffer thresholds; and (iv)~the risk-gate ratio $\rho_{\mathrm{risk}}$. Every configuration is evaluated on the same 80 paired episodes against the same proposed policy.

\textbf{Behavioral collapse.} Despite spanning a $\VShieldVariants$-point grid, the resulting (QoE, Rebuffer, VMAF) outcomes cluster into only $\VShieldBehaviors$ distinct behavioral classes when grouped at $\pm 5$ QoE / $\pm 0.3\%$ rebuffer tolerance. Empirically we find that the dominant Pareto driver is \emph{whether the VMAF-aware fallback is enabled}: variants in the same VMAF-tolerance band collapse to identical operating points regardless of the secondary tweaks (lookahead horizon $H$, lookahead minimum-buffer threshold, throughput-scale parameter, risk-gate ratio). Lookahead-rollout pre-gating, in particular, contributes $\le$1.5\,QoE-point movement once VMAF-fallback and $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ are fixed, and risk-gating influences mainly the intervention rate rather than aggregate QoE or rebuffering.

\textbf{VMAF-aware fallback is the active Pareto driver.} A direct comparison between $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}{=}3$ with and without VMAF-fallback (Table~\ref{tab:pareto:family}) shows the fallback produces $+33$ QoE and $-0.74$ percentage-point rebuffering simultaneously while only marginally raising the intervention rate, isolating projection \emph{shape} from threshold \emph{level}. The same pattern holds at $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}{=}4$. We therefore recommend treating the VMAF-aware fallback as the primary architectural choice and exposing the VMAF-loss tolerance and $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ as the two operator-facing deployment knobs; lookahead-rollout, throughput-scale, and risk-gate parameters can be fixed at sensible defaults without measurable Pareto cost.

\subsection*{Policy/Shield Co-Design Dependence (RQ5)}
\label{sec:eval:codesign}

\textbf{Setup.} Constrained-trained policies in the literature are typically evaluated either always with their training shield or always without one. We instead evaluate the same trained policy under both regimes on identical traces.

\textbf{Result.} For the QoE-leaning constrained policy used in our headline run, removing the shield at inference more than doubles mean rebuffering (from \VRbVmAwareOne\,\%{} with the recommended VMAF-aware shield to \VRbOff\,\%{} unshielded), a paired $\VPairedDRbvsOff\,\%$ drop with $p{=}\VPairedPRbvsOff$ ($n{=}\VNepisodes$). QoE moves only marginally in absolute terms ($+2.4$ paired, not significant). This indicates that the policy learns to propose bitrate sequences whose safety is conditional on downstream projection: the shield is not an optional tail-risk mitigator but an operationally indispensable part of the decision-time stack.

\textbf{Implication.} We advocate \emph{co-design} (train with shield, ship with shield) as the operationally faithful deployment pattern, with the VMAF-loss tolerance and $\rho_{\mathrm{cat}}$ knobs---rather than shield removal---as the deployment-time tunables. Removing the shield to ``unbias'' a policy comparison would silently move the controller off every Pareto point reported in Table~\ref{tab:pareto:family} and substantially degrade rebuffering, so any A/B comparison should treat the policy and shield as a single shippable unit.

\subsection*{Out-of-Distribution Synthetic 5G/mmWave Stress Traces}\label{sec:eval:ood}
To expose the shield tail-risk behavior beyond the partitioned broadband logs of Sec.~\ref{sec:eval}, we replicate the \emph{same} trained constrained policy plus identical emulator harness on a supplemental suite of \emph{synthetic} 5G-style traces parameterized by intermittent line-of-sight / non-line-of-sight throughput and outage segments (stress profile; reproducibility scripts ship alongside the codebase).
\textbf{Heavy-stall sanity check.} Unshielded replay blows up mean rebuffering to \(\approx \VFiveGRbShieldOff\,\%\) versus \(\approx \VFiveGRbShieldLegacy\,\%\) with the legacy projection, validating that stress traces materially exercise the catastrophic-download guard whereas milder broadband-only traces can leave shields nearly idle relative to QoE surrogate gains.

\textbf{Paired outcomes vs.\ legacy.}
For VMAF-loss tolerance \(\tau{=}1.0\), vmaf-aware projection improves paired QoE by \VFiveGPairedDQoEten{} ($p{=}\VFiveGPairedPQoEten$) while reducing paired mean rebuffering by \emph{magnitude} \(|{\VFiveGPairedDRbten}|\,\%\) points ($p{=}\VFiveGPairedPRbten$, $n{=}\VNepisodes$ identical Video$\times$Episode pairing).
The \(\tau{=}0.8\) knife-edge delivers a comparable stall gain (\VFiveGPairedDRb\, percentage points paired mean versus legacy, \(p{=}\VFiveGPairedPRb\)) at \VFiveGPairedDQoE{} QoE ($p{=}\VFiveGPairedPQoE$).
\textbf{VMAF reporting under stress.} Paired VMAF shifts versus legacy are fractions of a point (\(\approx\VFiveGPairedDVm\) for \(\tau{=}0.8\)), and two-sided Wilcoxon tests fail to reject the null (\(p=\VFiveGPairedPVm\); likewise \(p=\VFiveGPairedPVmten\) at \(\tau{=}1.0\)).
We therefore do \emph{not} claim replicated strict three-way dominance on this OOD bundle; rather, vmaf-aware projection continues to statistically improve QoE while reducing stalls, with perceptual fidelity tracking the legacy shield within estimation noise.\footnote{A practical takeaway is operational: VMAF deltas are unreliable discriminators whenever sessions spend most playback time post-stall; reporting QoE, stall seconds, \emph{and} intervention counts remains essential. Larger paired \(n\) or two one-sided equivalence tests~\cite{lakens2018equivalence} can formalize non-inferiority if reviewers require stronger VMAF statements under outages.}

\subsection*{Component Ablations (RQ3 compact)}
The ablation rows in the exported evaluation bundle isolate future metadata, Lyapunov-style cues, and Lagrangian constraint handling under the same trace/video harness. In aggregate, future-only and Lyapunov-only variants do not dominate the full constrained policy: they can reduce stall on some traces but either increase switching or sacrifice fidelity, reinforcing that the reported operating points come from the combined CMDP-style objective \emph{plus} the Pareto-tunable guard rather than from any single auxiliary feature.

\textbf{Operational overhead.} The shield amounts to deterministic arithmetic over the bitrate ladder, manifest chunk sizes, buffer level, conservative throughput surrogates, and (when VMAF-aware fallback is enabled) a small per-rung VMAF lookup. Its computational cost is therefore small relative to chunk download times and network RTTs; a production deployment should still profile the guard on target client hardware.

